name{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 true] [17592186221099 1100 "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s(s-1)}, \\qquad s > 1\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s-a},\\ s>a.$" 13194139536011 true] [17592186221099 1100 "For the function\n$G(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s(s-1)}, \\qquad s > 1$\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s)$, given that\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s-a},\\ s>a$." 13194139536011 false] [17592186221099 1145 "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as\n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{F(s)} &= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 1}} \\\\\n&= e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{G(s)} &= L^{-1}{(F(s))/(s)} \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{t}e^{τ}dτ \\\\\n&= e^{τ} \\mid _{0}^{t} \\\\\n&= e^{t} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 false] [17592186221099 1145 "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={e}^{τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 true] [17592186221104 1100 "Prove that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$" 13194139536011 true] [17592186221104 1100 "Prove that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}$." 13194139536011 false] [17592186221104 1145 "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that\n\n$$\nA'(t) = f(t)\n$$\n\nand $A(0) ={0}$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ &= A(τ) \\mid _{0}^{t} \\\\\n&= A(t) - A(0) \\\\\n&= A(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ} &= L{A(t)} = ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = A(t) & ⇒ & du = A^{′}(t)dt \\\\ dv = e^{-st}dt & ⇒ & v =-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A(t)dt &= A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}) \\mid _{0}^{∞} - ∫_{0}^{∞}(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st})A^{′}(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0\n$$\n\nas $t \\to \\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg \\mid _0^{\\infty} = {0}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t = \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using that\n\n$$\nA'(t) = f(t)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{s}∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A^{′}(t)dt &= \\frac{1}{s}∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s} \\cdot F(s) \\\\\n&= (F(s))/(s)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 false] [17592186221104 1145 "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} & =L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 true] [17592186221370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379298 13194139536011 true] [17592186221370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379305 13194139536011 true] [17592186221370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379312 13194139536011 true] [17592186221370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379319 13194139536011 true] [17592186221370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379326 13194139536011 true] [17592186221371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379291 13194139536011 true] [17592186221371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379333 13194139536011 true] [17592186221371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379340 13194139536011 true] [17592186221371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379347 13194139536011 true] [17592186221371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379354 13194139536011 true] [17592186221372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379384 1